WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.069
-My first guest
is one of my favorites.

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:05.305
She is an Oscar-, Emmy-,
and Tony-winning actress.

00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:09.708
She stars in "F9," which hits
theaters Friday, June 25th.

00:00:09.708 --> 00:00:12.011
Here is Helen Mirren!

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:13.480
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.981
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:14.981 --> 00:00:17.250
Oh, my gosh! Yes!

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.385
Oh, my gosh!

00:00:19.385 --> 00:00:24.857
You are the absolute --
You are the greatest ever!

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:27.960
Oh, my God!
That is the greatest.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.330
Oh my goodness! I love you!
Helen!

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:32.399
Helen --

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:34.233
-I'm in my favorite place
in the world.

00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:36.769
-Oh, my God!
You are the greatest.

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:38.338
I love that you're in a --
You might be

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:40.740
the first guest ever to be
in a bathtub on

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.308
"The Tonight Show" as a guest.

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:44.344
-Well, you know, that's
the brilliant thing about Zoom.

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:48.047
I was thinking, you know,
"Why not be in the place

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:50.216
that you love to
sit and chat to people?"

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:51.751
I love having a chat
to my husband

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.119
while I'm sitting in the bath.

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:55.221
So why not do it
to the whole of America?

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.389
-Yeah, that's very smart.
Why not?

00:00:57.389 --> 00:01:00.894
[ Cheers and applause ]
That's why you're the best.

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:01.928
I love --

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:03.329
-I'm running out of
bubbles, though.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.730
That's the problem.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:08.735
You know, you were chatting
away, and I was waiting

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:11.637
and bubbles were going down.

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:13.906
-Oh, I'll make the
interview very fast then, yeah.

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:16.209
-Alright, fast, please.
Fast and furious, yes.

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:17.443
-Fast and furious.

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:20.780
Oh, see? We already segued in.
Brilliant mind.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.548
I do want to ask you
some questions, though.

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:26.352
I love that you're in a bathtub.
It's so fun.

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:29.021
I follow you
on the social medias

00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:32.625
and I see that you're
very in tune with nature.

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:34.860
Wherever you live,
there's lots of creatures

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.998
and animals and squirrels and --
Well, squirrels, yeah.

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:46.939
-Chipmunks, golden mantles,
blue jays, coyotes,

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:49.675
and, of course, bears.

00:01:49.675 --> 00:01:53.979
-Now, I have a clip of you,
a black bear --

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:56.449
Helen Mirren
confronting a black bear.

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:57.750
Take a look at this.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.053
-Come. Come on here. No.
[ Knock on window ]

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:02.688
Go on.
-That's not going to stop it.

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:05.758
-Go on!

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:08.594
-You better watch out.
-No, he's fine.

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:10.663
Go on, go on! Go on, bear!
Go on!

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:13.866
Naughty bear! Naughty bear!
Naughty bear!

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:15.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:20.006
-"Naughty bear!
Naughty bear!"

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:22.075
Is yelling "naughty bear" --

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:24.477
is that exactly how
you get rid of a black bear?

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:25.845
-Well, it worked, didn't it?

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:27.413
And I've seen that bear since,

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:29.015
and every time --
I think it's a she.

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:30.516
I don't know if it is or not.

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:32.885
Every time I see her,
I go, "Naughty bear!

00:02:32.885 --> 00:02:34.420
Naughty bear!
Go on! Off you go!"

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.155
And off it goes.

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:37.623
-I think you should make
"Naughty Bear"

00:02:37.623 --> 00:02:39.325
into a children's book.

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.326
I would --
-"Naughty Bear," yes.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:41.727
-"Naughty Bear."
-Kids love that.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:42.728
-Yeah, why not?

00:02:42.728 --> 00:02:44.830
-Kids absolutely
love that video.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:45.898
-Yeah.
-Show it to your kids.

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:47.066
They love it,

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:49.735
'cause I think they get
told off so often.

00:02:49.735 --> 00:02:50.869
They're told that
they're naughty

00:02:50.869 --> 00:02:52.372
and they should
go to bed or something.

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:53.906
So they really recognize it.

00:02:53.906 --> 00:02:56.609
-Can you -- Tell me about
your show that you're doing,

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.077
speaking of nature.

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:01.380
It's called "When Nature Calls
with Helen Mirren."

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.083
I think it's really funny.
Great title.

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:06.585
But explain the premise, 'cause
that's what I really love.

00:03:06.585 --> 00:03:08.087
-It's a lovely idea.

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:11.023
It's all the off-cuts from these
great nature shows, you know,

00:03:11.023 --> 00:03:13.058
when they do these
incredible nature shows,

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:14.327
especially the BBC.

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:16.229
They have hours of film

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.497
that they can't use
in the actual program.

00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:21.867
But it's great footage.
I mean, incredible footage.

00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:26.439
So we've taken that footage and
we've sort of, you know,

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:28.507
given the animals voices,
you know,

00:03:28.507 --> 00:03:32.211
and made them sort of
understandable in their world.

00:03:32.211 --> 00:03:36.048
It's a way of putting ourselves
within the animal world

00:03:36.048 --> 00:03:37.883
and just showing that
we're all --

00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:39.985
We all share the same planet.

00:03:39.985 --> 00:03:41.020
It's very funny, too.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.087
-I think it's
really funny, yeah.

00:03:42.087 --> 00:03:43.622
I love the --
I love that premise.

00:03:43.622 --> 00:03:46.525
You know, I was researching you
for this interview.

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:48.127
Of course,
we've done many interviews.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:51.597
And I realized that
you're very close to an EGOT.

00:03:51.597 --> 00:03:53.732
You have an Emmy.

00:03:53.732 --> 00:03:57.603
You have an Oscar.
You're a Tony winner.

00:03:57.603 --> 00:03:59.138
And I think that next is --

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:00.339
I mean, do you want to
announce it tonight?

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:02.408
Are you dropping an album?
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:03.843
-I wish.

00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:07.280
I have a terrible disadvantage
where a Grammy is concerned,

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.848
which is,
I absolutely can't sing.

00:04:09.848 --> 00:04:13.152
I mean, I really, really,
really can't sing.

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:16.155
So, you know,
it's a terrible disadvantage

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:18.691
for an effort to get a Grammy.

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:22.161
Maybe I could do
a spoken-word album,

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:23.996
but that's as close as I'm ever
going to get to a Grammy.

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:25.731
-But then what is this?

00:04:25.731 --> 00:04:28.333
'Cause I found this photo
of you on the Internet.

00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:30.970
-Oh, that's from
a long time ago.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:34.440
Yes, that was a
theater show I was doing,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.975
and it was called
"Teeth 'n' Smiles."

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:37.243
And it was about --

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:39.779
It was sort of based on
Janis Joplin, in a way.

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:43.015
So I'm kind of
trying desperately to channel

00:04:43.015 --> 00:04:46.652
the great Janis Joplin there.

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:51.457
But I did sing onstage,
but, really, I shouted onstage,

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:55.461
sort of vaguely in tune,
but maybe not at all.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:56.863
But it was a great experience.

00:04:56.863 --> 00:04:59.131
-Was it, like,
a rock 'n' roll audience?

00:04:59.131 --> 00:05:00.399
-It was pretty rock 'n' roll.

00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:01.534
It was the first time
rock 'n' rollers

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:02.835
came to the theater, actually.

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:04.803
It's the first time
they'd ever done a play.

00:05:04.803 --> 00:05:07.439
And -- But I proved
the fact that

00:05:07.439 --> 00:05:11.209
I'm so not rock 'n' roll
by nature.

00:05:11.209 --> 00:05:14.179
You know, I'm a sort of
good theater girl by nature,

00:05:14.179 --> 00:05:16.649
rather than a
mad rock 'n' roller.

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:17.783
Because one night,

00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:20.386
Keith Moon sort of
appeared out of the blue.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:24.190
Well, actually, out of a rubbish
bin that he'd fallen into

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.925
in the alleyway
outside my dressing room.

00:05:26.925 --> 00:05:31.096
And I heard this huge kerfuffle,
you know, at the stage door,

00:05:31.096 --> 00:05:32.832
which was down below
my dressing room.

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:35.834
Coming up the stairs
outside my dressing room,

00:05:35.834 --> 00:05:37.336
and then bang, bang, bang
on the door.

00:05:37.336 --> 00:05:38.670
And I opened my door.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.673
And it was just before
my last entrance in the play.

00:05:41.673 --> 00:05:45.010
And, here, it was
Keith Moon standing there.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:46.479
He said, "'Ello, Helen."

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:49.281
I said, "Gosh, Keith, you know,
what an honor to meet you.

00:05:49.281 --> 00:05:50.349
How fabulous."

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:52.885
He said,
"I'm coming onstage with you.

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:54.286
It's supposed to be fantastic.

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:56.089
I'm coming onstage with you."

00:05:56.089 --> 00:06:00.692
And to my absolute discredit,
my rock 'n' roll discredit,

00:06:00.692 --> 00:06:04.063
I sort of got very English and,
"Oh, no, no, Keith.

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:05.631
You know, I don't think
that's a good idea."

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:07.567
He was completely blasted,
incidentally.

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:09.068
He was completely out of it.

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:15.174
So I did say no,
and it was a terrible mistake,

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:16.609
because, I mean, how --

00:06:16.609 --> 00:06:19.578
It wouldn't have mattered
how chaotic it had been.

00:06:19.578 --> 00:06:22.481
It would have been just such
a fabulous experience

00:06:22.481 --> 00:06:24.349
to be onstage with Keith Moon.

00:06:24.349 --> 00:06:26.752
-You would have been
a member of The Who, yeah.

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:28.687
-Absolutely, yeah.
-You know what?

00:06:28.687 --> 00:06:30.856
You're already a rock star.
You don't need it.

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:31.890
We love you, bud.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.425
I want to talk to you
about "F9,"

00:06:33.425 --> 00:06:35.695
the latest in the
"Fast and Furious" franchise.

00:06:35.695 --> 00:06:37.230
More with Helen Mirren
in a bathtub.

